    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyFirewallBlocking">There is no tunnel right now, so the programs of kill-switch rules are cut off from the internet by the Windows Firewall itself — the engine is not involved and these attempts will not appear in its log. The local network and Remote Desktop keep working.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyFirewallLifted">The tunnel is carrying traffic again — the firewall block is holding nothing back.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyKillSwitch">Blocked by the kill-switch of rule "{0}": {1}. The rule has no VPN option connected, so it stays sealed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyBlockedRule">Blocked by rule "{0}": {1}.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyBlockedBare">Blocked by engine rule {0} → REJECT. No such rule exists in the launcher — it may be left over from an earlier configuration.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_ItemTemplate">its template "{0}" matched {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_ItemSite">its address "{0}" matched {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_ItemProgram">its program "{0}" — all of that program's traffic goes through the rule</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_ItemCheckUrl">its check address "{0}" matched</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyDnsBlocked">The name didn’t resolve: no address came back from your computer’s DNS server — your router or provider. The launcher has no DNS of its own, and the rules aren’t involved ({0}).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyForeignDns">Not ours: another program asked for this name and your computer’s DNS server — router or provider — returned no address. The launcher has no DNS of its own ({0}).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyDnsStub">Your PC's DNS server (your ISP's or your router's) answered with the 0.0.0.0 stub — the domain is on its ad/tracker list. That's it doing its job, not a fault: rules and the kill-switch are not involved.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyForeignDirect">A direct connection (not through the VPN): no launcher rule covers it. The program itself failed to reach that address — the far side refused or stayed silent: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Log_WhyDnsViaNode">«{0}» could not resolve the name: the lookup happens at the far end of that tunnel and it never answered ({1}). So the VPN node is at fault, not this machine's DNS.</sys:String>
